Laboratory tests were as follows:
[ESR, WBC, absolute neutrophil count, CRP]: all within normal range;
[T-SPOT]: positive;
[PCT, IL6, endotoxin, G test]: all negative;
[Tumor markers: NSE, CYFRA21-1, CEA, CA199, CA125, SCC, ferritin]: all within normal range;
[Autoimmune tests, IgG4]: all negative;
[Routine biochemistry]: total protein: 61.8g/L ↓, albumin: 35.9g/L ↓, prealbumin: 387mg/L ↑, uric acid: 406umol/L ↑, CK: 25U/L ↓;
[Glycated hemoglobin]: 6.3% ↑;
[Coagulation function, T-lymphocyte subsets]: no obvious abnormality;
[Urinalysis]: occult blood +-, urine red blood cell count 12.8/ul;
Imaging and related pathogen results, as well as some pathology results, were reported as follows:
[Electronic bronchoscopy]: mild luminal stenosis of the dorsal segment of the right lower lobe; BALF was performed in the dorsal segment of the right lower lobe and submitted for bacterial, fungal, acid-fast, and cell block testing.
[BALF bacterial and fungal cultures and acid-fast stain]: no abnormalities detected;
[BALF cell block]: cell block preparation failed; no cellular components were seen;
[Endobronchial ultrasound]: under the ultrasound interface, a submucosal soft tissue echogenic lesion was seen below the opening of the dorsal segment of the right lower lobe, relatively large in size; TBNA was performed twice, and aspirated tissue was sent for cytology smear, routine analysis of puncture fluid, mNGS testing, and pathology. Conclusion: submucosal soft tissue density lesion at the opening of the dorsal segment of the right lower lobe. Cytology smear of EBUS-aspirated tissue showed no malignant cells. Routine analysis of puncture fluid from EBUS-aspirated tissue showed: appearance: colorless, slightly turbid; specific gravity: scant; Rivalta test (-); red blood cell count: 0X10^6/L; white blood cell count: 90X10^6/L; segmented neutrophils 43%, lymphocytes 26%, monocytes 31%; mNGS of EBUS-aspirated tissue showed: Staphylococcus aureus (sequence count 13), Rhizopus arrhizus (sequence count 1).
Non-contrast chest CT with 3D reconstruction showed: 1. Right lung lesion (patchy consolidation and ground-glass opacity in the right lung, more prominent in the right lower lung, with slightly indistinct margins, with partial bronchiectasis and punctate calcifications within), inflammatory process considered possible; follow-up reexamination recommended after treatment; 2. Additional small nodules in both lungs, annual follow-up recommended.
